1. Problem Statement

To develop an AVL Tree Traversal System using C++. 
The system constructs a self-balancing binary search tree from user-entered integers.
It displays the inorder, preorder, postorder, and level-order traversals. 
The project demonstrates AVL tree insertion, balance factor calculation, tree rotations, and different tree traversal techniques.

2. Approach

The program uses an AVL tree, which is a self-balancing binary search tree. 
Each new integer is inserted according to binary search tree rules.
After insertion, the height and balance factor of each affected node are updated. 
If the tree becomes unbalanced, left or right rotations are performed.
The four traversals are implemented as follows:

1.Inorder: Visits the left subtree, root, and right subtree.
2.Preorder: Visits the root, left subtree, and right subtree.
3.Postorder: Visits the left subtree, right subtree, and root.
4.Level-order: Visits nodes level by level using a queue.

3. Complexity Analysis

Let n be the number of nodes in the AVL tree.

 Operation                            Time Complexity

Insertion of one node                    O(log n)

Construction of the tree                 O(n log n)

Inorder traversal                          O(n)

Preorder traversal                         O(n)

Postorder traversal                         O(n)

Level-order traversal                       O(n)

Space complexity                             O(n)


4. Conclusion

The AVL Tree Traversal System successfully demonstrates the implementation of a self-balancing binary search tree in C++.
It allows users to insert integers and display four different traversals. 
The project helps in understanding tree rotations, balance factors, recursion, queues, and the importance
of maintaining a balanced tree for efficient operations.
